Parallel lines have equal gradients: $m_1=m_2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.0,-1.8) -- (2.2,3.4) node[above left] {$m_1$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-2.2,-3.4) -- (3.0,1.8) node[above left] {$m_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

When both gradients are defined, perpendicular lines have gradients whose product is $-1$: $m_1\times m_2=-1$. A horizontal line and a vertical line are also perpendicular; for example, $y=3$ and $x=3$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-1.35,-3.4) -- (2.05,3.4) node[above left] {$m_1$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (3.4,-0.9) -- (-3.4,2.5) node[above left] {$m_2$};
\coordinate (P) at (0.6,0.5);
\coordinate (A) at (2.05,3.4);
\coordinate (B) at (-3.4,2.5);
\coordinate (ra) at ($(P)!0.32cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(P)!0.32cm!(B)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(P)$) -- (rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Flipping without changing the sign gives a product of $+1$, so $5$ and $\frac{1}{5}$ are **neither**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
